PLACES=[
N('seven-gables-salem','House of the Seven Gables (Turner-Ingersoll Mansion)','Salem','nshore','landmark','standing',42.52177,-70.88383,'exact','open','drive','','1668',
  'Built in 1668 for Captain John Turner, a Salem sea captain and merchant, and enlarged by him by 1676; his son John Turner II remodeled it in the Georgian style in the early 1700s. Nathaniel Hawthorne\'s 1851 novel made it famous. Caroline Emmerton bought it in 1908 and restored it, rebuilding missing gables, and opened it in 1910 as a museum whose fees support a settlement house that still runs classes for immigrants.',
  look='One of the oldest timber-framed mansion houses on the continent, 17 rooms facing Salem Harbor. Four windows of the original 1668 ground-floor room survive in a side wall; inside are original beams and rafters, Georgian paneling, and the secret staircase Emmerton added behind a closet. Nathaniel Hawthorne\'s birthplace, moved here from Union Street, stands beside it on the same ticket.',
  accn='Run by the House of the Seven Gables Settlement Association; admission charged for tours (its visit page, read November 2026: open daily, closed Thanksgiving). 115 Derby Street. Parking not checked: city lots on Derby and Blaney streets are mapped nearby.',
  links=[W('House of the Seven Gables'),L('The House of the Seven Gables: Visit','https://7gables.org/visit/')],
  hours='Daily 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; closed Thanksgiving',brick='some'),
N('witch-house-salem','The Witch House (Jonathan Corwin House)','Salem','nshore','landmark','standing',42.52155,-70.89890,'exact','open','drive','','1675',
  'Judge Jonathan Corwin bought this unfinished house in 1675 and lived here more than forty years. He sat on the Court of Oyer and Terminer in 1692, which sent 19 people to the gallows, and accused people were brought here for examination. It is one of the few buildings still standing in Salem with direct ties to the trials. Saved from street widening about 1940, it was moved back from the road and restored to a presumed 17th-century look in 1945.',
  look='A first-period house with steep gables and a central chimney, as restored in 1945; the facade gables had been removed and a gambrel roof added in 1746-47, so what you see outside is partly reconstruction. Inside, self-guided rooms on the trials and 17th-century life.',
  accn='Owned and run by the City of Salem as a museum, 310 Essex Street, at North Street. Hours from its own site, which loads by script and showed one line (November 2026). Admission charged. Parking not checked: city garages downtown.',
  links=[W('The Witch House'),L('The Witch House','https://www.thewitchhouse.org/')],
  hours='Thursday to Sunday 12 to 4 p.m. (as posted November 2026)',brick='some'),
N('gardner-pingree-house','Gardner-Pingree House','Salem','nshore','landmark','standing',42.52250,-70.89120,'exact','open','drive','','1804',
  'Samuel McIntire built this house in 1804 for John and Sarah Gardner; it is counted a masterpiece of Federal architecture and was made a National Historic Landmark in 1972. The killing of Joseph White in this house is one of the murders PEM\'s tours tell of. The Peabody Essex Museum owns it.',
  look='Three stories of Flemish-bond brick with white marble bands, a balustraded roof and an elliptical portico on four Corinthian columns. Inside, carved mantels, cornices, window shutters and stair balustrades by McIntire\'s shop on the first floor.',
  accn='Owned by the '+PEM+', 128 Essex Street. The inside is seen only on PEM\'s guided walking tours (its tour page, read November 2026, says they run July 2 to November 6 and are finished for 2026). PEM names the Museum Place garage on New Liberty Street for parking.',
  links=[W('Gardner–Pingree House'),PEMW],
  hours='Inside by PEM guided walking tour only, July to early November',brick='yes'),
N('john-ward-house-salem','John Ward House','Salem','nshore','landmark','standing',42.52290,-70.89300,'site','open','drive','','1684',
  'A currier, John Ward, built the first part in 1684 and enlarged it twice; the rear lean-to that gives it a saltbox shape came not long before his death in 1734. George Francis Dow restored it in the early 1900s, and in 1911 it opened as the first outdoor museum of architecture in the country. Now owned by the Peabody Essex Museum; a National Historic Landmark since 1968.',
  look='A two-and-a-half-story clapboard first-period house with a big central chimney and plain trim. PEM\'s tour text says it stood within sight of the hangings on Gallows Hill (it has been moved since, as part of the museum\'s grounds; when is not checked). Interior first-period framing; rooms not checked.',
  accn='Owned by the '+PEM+'; listed at 9 Brown Street, inside the museum\'s garden block. Inside only on PEM\'s guided walking tours, July to early November (tour page read November 2026). Seen from Brown Street at other times.',
  links=[W('John Ward House (Salem, Massachusetts)'),PEMW],
  hours='Inside by PEM guided walking tour only, July to early November',brick='no'),
N('ropes-mansion-salem','Ropes Mansion and garden','Salem','nshore','landmark','standing',42.52121,-70.89977,'exact','open','drive','','late 1720s',
  'A Georgian merchant\'s house built in the late 1720s for Samuel Barnard, a survivor of the 1704 Deerfield raid; no tree-ring study has fixed the date. The Ropes family later owned it; it was moved back from Essex Street in the 1800s and given an Ionic entrance and rear ell. The Peabody Essex Museum runs it; the formal garden behind was laid out in 1912.',
  look='A white clapboard house with a gambrel roof behind a white wooden fence. Inside, Colonial Revival rooms and the family\'s furnishings on a self-guided tour; the 1912 garden and arbor lie behind. It was the house in the 1993 film Hocus Pocus.',
  accn='Run by the '+PEM+', 318 Essex Street. Timed ticket, not part of museum admission. PEM\'s pages (read November 2026): tours Saturdays and Sundays, May 23 to October 18 in 2026; closed for the 2026 season now. Garden opening not checked.',
  links=[W('Ropes Mansion'),PEMW,L('PEM: Visit','https://www.pem.org/visit')],
  hours='Saturday and Sunday, late May to mid-October (self-guided, timed ticket)',brick='no'),
N('peirce-nichols-house','Peirce-Nichols House','Salem','nshore','landmark','standing',42.52288,-70.89958,'exact','view','drive','','c. 1782–1784',
  'An early work of the Salem builder Samuel McIntire for Jerathmiel Peirce, a leather dresser who grew rich in privateering and the East India trade; McIntire later remodeled part of it. It became a National Historic Landmark in 1973 as an early masterwork of one of the country\'s first recognized master builders, and is owned by the Peabody Essex Museum.',
  look='A three-story clapboard house with a pedimented center and flanking round-arched openings, patterned closely on Batty Langley\'s 1740 builder\'s book. Opened to the public in the late 1930s; PEM\'s 2026 tour pages do not list it, so interior not checked.',
  accn='Owned by the '+PEM+', 80 Federal Street. Look from the street; no current visiting hours were found on PEM\'s pages (November 2026). Dates are Wikipedia\'s (begun about 1782, assessed as finished in 1784, east parlor and hall redone in 1801); not checked against the Register form.',
  links=[W('Peirce–Nichols House')],
  hours='?',brick='no',conf='mixed'),
N('east-india-marine-hall','East India Marine Hall (Peabody Essex Museum)','Salem','nshore','civic','standing',42.52149,-70.89223,'site','open','drive','','1824–1825',
  'Built in 1824-25 for the East India Marine Society (founded 1799) to house its collection of maritime material. It holds the nation\'s oldest continuously operating museum collection and has been a National Historic Landmark since 1965. It is now wrapped into the modern Peabody Essex Museum.',
  look='The granite Essex Street front survives, with six square bays below and seven tall round-arched windows above; the sides are brick. Upstairs the old ballroom is still one large open gallery, though much of its historic finish was lost in 1860s alterations.',
  accn='Part of the '+PEM+', 161 Essex Street; general admission (PEM\'s visit page, read November 2026). Museum Place garage on New Liberty Street.',
  links=[W('Peabody Museum of Salem'),L('PEM: Visit','https://www.pem.org/visit')],
  hours='Thursday to Monday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed Tuesday and Wednesday',brick='?'),
N('salem-custom-house','Salem Custom House','Salem','nshore','civic','standing',42.52186,-70.88701,'exact','open','drive','','1819',
  'Salem had a custom house from 1649; this, the last of thirteen, was built in 1819 and served the U.S. Customs Service into the 1930s. Nathaniel Hawthorne worked here for three years, and the building opens The Scarlet Letter. It is part of Salem Maritime National Historical Park.',
  look='A red-brick building on a granite base with a gilded replica eagle on the roof (the 1826 wooden original by Joseph True is inside). High ceilings, a sweeping staircase and carved woodwork; customs offices furnished as in long use, and Hawthorne\'s office. The Public Stores warehouse is attached at the back.',
  accn='National Park Service, Salem Maritime National Historical Park, 178 Derby Street; free. The park\'s hours page (updated November 3, 2026): Wednesday to Sunday 9:30 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. until November 14, then closed for the season to April 30. Grounds open around the clock.',
  links=[L('NPS: Custom House','https://www.nps.gov/places/custom-house.htm'),NPSH,W('Salem Maritime National Historical Park')],
  hours='Wednesday to Sunday 9:30 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. into mid-November; closed November 15 to April 30',brick='yes'),
N('derby-house-salem','Derby House','Salem','nshore','landmark','standing',42.52186,-70.88668,'exact','view','drive','','1762',
  'Built in 1762 as a wedding present for Elias Hasket Derby and Elizabeth Crowninshield Derby, who lived here twenty years with seven children and at least two enslaved people. Derby turned family ships into privateers in the Revolution and became one of the richest men in the country. Later a tenement for Polish mill families, it was restored by the Society for the Preservation of New England Antiquities and given to the park in 1937.',
  look='The "little brick house," as Derby called it: a Georgian brick house facing Derby Wharf, with the West India Goods Store of the early 1800s beside it. Interior not checked: it is not on the park\'s November 2026 hours list.',
  accn='National Park Service, Salem Maritime National Historical Park, Derby Street. Seen from the street and the park grounds, which are open around the clock; whether and when the house opens was not found.',
  links=[L('NPS: Derby House','https://www.nps.gov/places/derby-house.htm'),NPSH],
  hours='?',brick='yes'),
N('narbonne-house-salem','Narbonne House','Salem','nshore','landmark','standing',42.52265,-70.88738,'exact','open','drive','','1675',
  'Built in 1675 for a butcher, Thomas Ives, and lived in for nearly 300 years, almost 200 of them by one family; Sarah Narbonne was born here and died here in 1895 aged 101. The National Park Service bought it in 1963.',
  look='The taller part is the 1675 house, with its original peaked roof and original brick chimney; a kitchen lean-to and a side addition came later. It is kept as an architectural study house, not restored to one date, so the changes over three centuries show.',
  accn='National Park Service, Salem Maritime National Historical Park, 71 Essex Street; free. Hours from the park\'s hours page (updated November 3, 2026), which gives Wednesday to Sunday 1 to 4 p.m. and no winter line.',
  links=[L('NPS: Narbonne House','https://www.nps.gov/places/narbonne-house.htm'),NPSH],
  hours='Wednesday to Sunday 1 to 4 p.m. (autumn 2026; winter not stated)',brick='some'),
N('derby-wharf-light','Derby Wharf and Derby Wharf Light','Salem','nshore','light','standing',42.51656,-70.88358,'exact','public','walk','','1762; light 1871',
  'Richard Derby and his son began the wharf in 1762 and stretched it to 2,045 feet by 1806, Salem\'s longest, once lined with nearly twenty buildings. The small light at its end was built in 1871 and is still lit, now by the sun. Both are part of Salem Maritime National Historical Park.',
  look='A walk of about 600 m out the granite-faced wharf (the 1762 core was timber cribs filled with stones, found in a 1992 dig) to a small white square tower 25 feet above high water. The replica East Indiaman Friendship of Salem docks along it.',
  accn='National Park Service; the wharf and park grounds are open 24 hours a day, every day (park pages read November 2026). Free. Start from Derby Street opposite the Custom House.',
  links=[W('Derby Wharf Light'),L('NPS: Derby Wharf','https://www.nps.gov/places/derby-wharf.htm'),L('NPS: Things to do','https://www.nps.gov/sama/planyourvisit/things2do.htm')],
  hours='Wharf and grounds open 24 hours',brick='?'),
N('hamilton-hall-salem','Hamilton Hall','Salem','nshore','civic','standing',42.51948,-70.89928,'exact','view','drive','','1805–1807',
  'Samuel McIntire designed this hall in 1805-07 as a social space for Salem\'s leading families and named it for Alexander Hamilton. It is still a social hall, used for balls, weddings and meetings, and a National Historic Landmark.',
  look='A three-story Flemish-bond brick building at Chestnut and Cambridge streets, with a Greek Revival porch of about 1845 on the Cambridge Street end. Interior not checked.',
  accn='Privately run hall, 9 Chestnut Street; look from the street. Wikipedia says a lecture series is held there on Thursdays in February and March; not checked against the hall\'s own page.',
  links=[W('Hamilton Hall (Salem, Massachusetts)')],
  brick='yes'),
N('charter-street-cemetery-salem','Charter Street Cemetery (Old Burying Point)','Salem','nshore','burial','standing',42.52037,-70.89221,'site','public','walk','cemeteries','1637',
  'Salem\'s oldest burying ground, in use since at least 1637. Two judges of the 1692 witch-trial court lie here, Bartholomew Gedney and John Hathorne, great-great-grandfather of Nathaniel Hawthorne, as does Mary Corey, Giles Corey\'s second wife. The victims were never given proper burials; the Salem Witch Trials Memorial beside it has a bench for each.',
  look='Headstones of the 1600s and 1700s on a small rectangular plot hemmed in by downtown. The Pickman House at the gate, a first-period house of about 1665 (the Salem Heritage Trail gives 1672), is the cemetery\'s welcome center; the Grimshawe House, where Hawthorne courted Sophia Peabody, stands on the other side.',
  accn='City of Salem cemetery on Charter Street; the welcome center is run with the Peabody Essex Museum. Staff hours from Destination Salem\'s 2021 page; the City\'s own cemetery pages would not open, so gate hours and the City\'s October visitor limits are not checked.',
  links=[W('Charter Street Historic District'),L('Destination Salem: Charter Street Cemetery Welcome Center','https://www.salem.org/blog/visit-the-charter-street-cemetery-welcome-center-in-salem-massachusetts/'),L('Salem Heritage Trail: Charter Street Cemetery','https://salemheritagetrail.org/locations/charter-street-cemetery/')],
  hours='Welcome center staff daily 10 a.m. to 6 p.m. in summer, 12 to 4 p.m. November 16 to May 14 (2021 page)',brick='?',conf='mixed'),
N('pioneer-village-salem','Pioneer Village (Salem 1630)','Salem','nshore','town','standing',42.50793,-70.88573,'site','open','drive','','1930',
  'Built in 1930 for the Massachusetts tercentenary by George Francis Dow and the architect Joseph Everett Chandler, with grounds planned by Harlan Page Kelsey: the first living history museum in the country. It shows Salem as English settlers might have built it in 1630. The City of Salem runs it with the Witch House.',
  look='Thatched cottages, dugouts, a wigwam, a blacksmith\'s shop, a sawmill, saltworks and gardens on about three acres of Forest River Park, and the Governor\'s "Faire House," Dow\'s idea of Governor Endicott\'s 1628 house. The buildings are 1930 recreations, themselves nearly a century old.',
  accn='City of Salem, in Forest River Park off West Avenue. Season and hours from pioneervillagesalem.org (read November 2026; closed for the season now). Parking: the Forest River Park lot to the south.',
  links=[W('Pioneer Village (Salem, Massachusetts)'),L('Pioneer Village','https://pioneervillagesalem.org')],
  th=[42.50474,-70.88448],thn='Forest River Park lot',hours='Saturday and Sunday 12 to 4 p.m., early June to mid-November',brick='?'),
N('fort-pickering-salem','Fort Pickering and Winter Island Light','Salem','nshore','fort','ruins',42.52713,-70.86699,'site','public','walk','forts','1643',
  'Fortified from 1643 as Fort William, renamed Fort Anne about 1702, rebuilt for the Revolution, and rebuilt by the new federal government in 1794 and named for Timothy Pickering in 1799; the 1811 fort was brick and sod with six guns. In the Civil War it was enlarged with earthwork parapets, a bigger magazine and a moat. From 1935 to 1971 Winter Island was a Coast Guard seaplane station. The lighthouse beside the fort dates from 1871.',
  look='Portions of the Civil War fort: earthwork parapets and three 19th-century ammunition bunkers, granite-faced doorways set into the earth and barred (Wikipedia\'s photographs). The small Fort Pickering (Winter Island) Light of 1871 stands on the rocks below, discontinued in 1969 and relit by the City in 1983. The abandoned Coast Guard seaplane hangar and barracks are in the same park.',
  accn='Winter Island Maritime Park, City of Salem, at the end of Winter Island Road off Fort Avenue: a public park with a beach, campground and boat ramp (Destination Salem\'s listing, read November 2026; RV and tent camping mid-May to October). Day hours and parking fees not checked: the City\'s park pages would not open.',
  links=[W('Fort Pickering'),W('Fort Pickering Light'),W('Winter Island'),L('Destination Salem: Winter Island Park','https://salem.org/listing/winter-island-park')],
  th=[42.52719,-70.87027],thn='Winter Island Maritime Park lot',hours='?',brick='?'),
N('fort-lee-salem','Fort Lee','Salem','nshore','fort','traces',42.53212,-70.87419,'site','public','walk','forts','1776 (or 1742)',
  'A star-shaped earthwork on the heights of Salem Neck, one of the few Revolutionary-era earthwork forts left in Massachusetts in close to its first shape. Wikipedia dates the works to 1776, with a garrison of 100 artillerymen and 16 guns; historyofmassachusetts.org says gun platforms were first built here in 1742. It was rebuilt for the Civil War and garrisoned in 1898, and passed to the City of Salem in 1922.',
  look='Overgrown earth ramparts and a stone magazine. Wikipedia describes an irregular five-pointed star, historyofmassachusetts.org a four-pointed star with a gate in the west rampart. Paths and signs were made in 1976 and later taken away; a scout troop cleared the trails in 2014.',
  accn='City of Salem land beside Fort Avenue on Salem Neck (owner from Wikipedia; no City page read). No signs or lot: park legally along Fort Avenue or at Winter Island and walk. Expect brush and ticks.',
  links=[W('Fort Lee (Salem, Massachusetts)'),L('History of Massachusetts: Fort Lee','https://historyofmassachusetts.org/fort-lee-salem/')],
  hours='?',brick='no',conf='mixed'),
N('bakers-island-light','Bakers Island Light Station','Salem','nshore','light','standing',42.53644,-70.78592,'exact','boat','boat','','1820',
  'A light was kept on Bakers Island from 1798, first two lights on the keeper\'s house; an octagonal stone tower followed in 1815 and the present round stone tower in 1820. The two towers were nicknamed Ma and Pa until the shorter was removed in 1926. Essex National Heritage Commission now owns the light station.',
  look='A round stone tower with the keepers\' houses on a rocky island at the mouth of Salem Sound. Seen from the ferry or on a tour; Essex Heritage also offers overnight stays in the assistant keeper\'s house.',
  accn='Light station owned by Essex Heritage; the rest of the island, the Bakers Island Wharf included, is private, so land only on a tour. Essex Heritage\'s page (read November 2026): summer boat tours on the Naumkeag from the Salem Ferry Dock, 10 Blaney Street. Tour days not checked.',
  links=[W('Bakers Island Light'),L('Essex Heritage: Bakers Island Light Station','https://essexheritage.org/bakers')],
  th=[42.52267,-70.88238],thn='Blaney Street lot by the Salem Ferry Dock',hours='?',brick='?'),
N('fort-sewall-marblehead','Fort Sewall','Marblehead','nshore','fort','ruins',42.50864,-70.84151,'exact','public','walk','forts','1644',
  'Gale\'s Head was first fortified in 1644, one of the oldest English coastal works in the country; the fort was rebuilt in 1742, 1775, 1794, 1799 and 1809. In April 1814 the USS Constitution sheltered under its guns from two British frigates, which turned away. Named that year for Chief Justice Samuel Sewall, it fell into ruin, was doubled in size in the Civil War, and has been a town park since the federal government handed it over in 1922.',
  look='Grassy ramparts on the point at the mouth of Marblehead Harbor, with the magazines of the 1794 and 1799 rebuildings. A restoration was finished in 2022 (Daily Item, June 2022); what it changed is not checked.',
  accn='Town of Marblehead park at the end of Front Street (Wikipedia). The town\'s parks page answered 403, so hours and rules are not checked. OpenStreetMap maps a small lot on Fort Beach Lane just west of the fort; on the aerial it could not be told from the road, so it is not given as the parking point.',
  links=[W('Fort Sewall'),L('Daily Item: Fort Sewall restoration celebration (2022)','https://itemlive.com/2022/06/16/fort-sewall-restoration-celebration-set-for-saturday')],
  hours='?',brick='?'),
N('jeremiah-lee-mansion','Jeremiah Lee Mansion','Marblehead','nshore','landmark','standing',42.50362,-70.85126,'exact','open','drive','','1768',
  'Built in 1768 for Colonel Jeremiah Lee, then the richest merchant and shipowner in the province, and one of the largest and finest houses of late-colonial America. A National Historic Landmark since 1960, it is the Marblehead Museum\'s house museum.',
  look='A big wooden Georgian house cut and painted to look like stone ashlar. Inside, kept in a nearly original state: rare 18th-century English hand-painted wallpapers, the only ones still in place, rococo carving, a mahogany-paneled hall and staircase, and furniture by Boston, Salem and Marblehead makers. A garden behind.',
  accn='Marblehead Museum, 161 Washington Street. Its pages (read November 2026): open June 1 to October 31, 45-minute tours on the hour; admission charged, free for children under 14 and some others. Parking not checked: street parking in the old town.',
  links=[W('Jeremiah Lee Mansion'),L('Marblehead Museum: Lee Mansion','https://marbleheadmuseum.org/properties/lee-mansion/'),L('Marblehead Museum: Hours','https://marbleheadmuseum.org/hours/')],
  hours='June to October, Tuesday to Saturday; tours on the hour 10 a.m. to 3 p.m.',brick='no'),
N('abbot-hall-marblehead','Abbot Hall','Marblehead','nshore','civic','standing',42.50259,-70.85239,'exact','open','drive','','1876–1877',
  'Marblehead\'s fourth town hall, built in 1876-77 with money left by Benjamin Abbot, a cooper and trader, to a Romanesque design by Lord & Fuller. It is still the seat of the town government and doubles as a museum.',
  look='The tower holds an 1877 Howard clock that workers still climb up to wind every week. The Selectmen\'s Room holds the original Spirit of \'76 painting by Archibald Willard and the 1684 deed to Marblehead signed by descendants of Wenepoykin, youngest son of the sachem Nanepashemet; displays line the halls, and a maritime museum is on the first floor.',
  accn='Town of Marblehead, 188 Washington Street. The Historical Commission\'s page says the Selectmen\'s Room and displays are open whenever the hall is open, and gives the gift shop\'s hours (used here); free on-street parking is usually available. The town\'s own office hours not checked.',
  links=[W('Abbot Hall (Marblehead, Massachusetts)'),L('Marblehead Historical Commission: Visit Abbot Hall','https://marbleheadhistory.org/?p=46')],
  hours='Monday to Thursday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m., Friday 10 to 12:30; summer weekends too (gift shop hours)',brick='yes'),
N('st-michaels-marblehead','St. Michael\'s Church','Marblehead','nshore','worship','standing',42.50460,-70.85088,'exact','open','drive','','1714',
  'Built in 1714 for the Church of England, most of its donors sea captains, it is New England\'s oldest Episcopal church still on its first site. It was enlarged in 1728, and in 1776 a crowd of patriots tore the royal coat of arms from it. The rotten spire came down in 1793; a new one was put up for the 300th anniversary in 2014.',
  look='The original church was square, like the Nieuwe Kerk in Haarlem in layout and roof, Wikipedia says. The 1833 renovation brought Gothic windows, new pews and the altar, pulpit and original reredos at the north end. Interior beyond that not checked.',
  accn='Active Episcopal parish; the address is on Pleasant Street, though the church faces Summer Street. Service times from its worship page (read November 2026). A thrift shop opens Fridays and Saturdays 11 to 3 (its FAQ).',
  links=[W("St. Michael's Church (Marblehead, Massachusetts)"),L("St. Michael's: Worship",'https://www.stmichaels1714.org/worship')],
  hours='Sundays 8 and 10 a.m. (one 9 a.m. service in July and August); Evensong 5 p.m. first Sunday, October to May',brick='no'),
N('old-town-house-marblehead','Old Town House','Marblehead','nshore','civic','standing',42.50529,-70.84964,'exact','view','drive','','1727',
  'Built in 1727 to replace the old meeting house: a town hall upstairs and a market below. Elbridge Gerry and John Glover argued for independence here. Abbot Hall replaced it as the town hall in 1876.',
  look='A pale clapboard building over a granite basement, dated 1727 over the door, with a brick chimney (Wikipedia\'s photograph), at the head of Market Square, where Washington, State and Mugford streets meet. Upstairs is still used as a town hall; the ground floor holds the Marblehead Police Museum. Interior and museum hours not checked.',
  accn='Town of Marblehead, Market Square. Look from the square.',
  links=[W('Old Town House (Marblehead, Massachusetts)')],
  brick='some'),
N('old-burial-hill-marblehead','Old Burial Hill','Marblehead','nshore','burial','standing',42.51066,-70.84642,'site','public','walk','cemeteries','1638',
  'Marblehead\'s burying ground since 1638, on the high ground where the town\'s first meeting house stood. It holds Puritan gravestones of the 1600s with varied carving and a memorial stone for Wilmot Redd, a Marblehead woman hanged in the 1692 witch trials.',
  look='Old gravestones on the hill, with views over the harbor and Salem Sound. The daytime cemetery scenes of Hocus Pocus (1993) were filmed here.',
  accn='Reached on foot by the walkway at Redd\'s Pond or the stairs at Orne and Pond streets (Wikipedia). Owner and hours not checked; treat as a daylight visit. No lot: street parking in the old town.',
  links=[W('Old Burial Hill (Marblehead, Massachusetts)')],
  hours='?',brick='?',conf='mixed'),
]
